Each flag can silently block authentication even when credentials are correct, and each throws a different `AuthenticationException`:

**`isEnabled()`**
- Returns `false` -> throws `DisabledException`
- Use for soft-deleted or email-unverified accounts

**`isAccountNonExpired()`**
- Returns `false` -> throws `AccountExpiredException`
- Use for time-limited accounts (contractors, trial users)

**`isAccountNonLocked()`**
- Returns `false` -> throws `LockedException`
- Use for brute-force lockout

**`isCredentialsNonExpired()`**
- Returns `false` -> throws `CredentialsExpiredException`
- Use for mandatory password rotation policies

**Why all errors look the same to clients:**
- `DaoAuthenticationProvider` converts all four exceptions to a generic `"Bad credentials"` message
- This is intentional — prevents user enumeration
- From inside the logs you can still distinguish them

**Map flags to real entity columns:**

```java
public class AppUserDetails implements UserDetails {
    private final AppUser user;

    @Override
    public boolean isEnabled() { return user.isActive(); }

    @Override
    public boolean isAccountNonLocked() { return !user.isLocked(); }

    @Override
    public boolean isCredentialsNonExpired() {
        return user.getPasswordExpiresAt() == null ||
               user.getPasswordExpiresAt().isAfter(Instant.now());
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isAccountNonExpired() { return true; }
}
```

**Operational benefit:**
- Flipping `user.setLocked(true)` in the DB instantly blocks the account on next login
- No application code change, no redeploy

**Implement the loader:**

```java
@Service
public class DatabaseUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {
    private final UserRepository userRepo;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) {
        AppUser user = userRepo.findByEmail(username)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found: " + username));
        return new AppUserDetails(user);
    }
}
```

**The `ROLE_` prefix is the #1 silent failure source:**
- `hasRole("ADMIN")` internally looks for `ROLE_ADMIN`
- Without the prefix, the user authenticates but every `hasRole()` call returns `false`
- Use `hasAuthority("ADMIN")` if you don't want the prefix convention

**Build authorities correctly:**

```java
@Override
public Collection<? extends GrantedAuthority> getAuthorities() {
    return user.getRoles().stream()
        .map(role -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + role.getName()))
        .toList();
}
```

**Auto-wiring rules:**
- Spring Boot auto-detects a single `UserDetailsService` bean and wires it into `DaoAuthenticationProvider`
- For multiple beans (e.g., admin vs customer auth), configure `DaoAuthenticationProvider` explicitly
- Provide a `PasswordEncoder` bean too — without it, Spring Boot 6 throws on startup

**Session-based auth** stores the user's identity server-side in an HTTP session; the cookie (`JSESSIONID`) is just a pointer. **Token-based auth** (typically JWT) is stateless — the token itself carries the identity and is validated on every request.

```java
// -------- SESSION-BASED (traditional web apps) --------
@Bean SecurityFilterChain sessionChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated())
        .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults())
        .sessionManagement(s -> s
            .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.IF_REQUIRED)
            .maximumSessions(1).maxSessionsPreventsLogin(true))      // single sign-on
        .rememberMe(r -> r.key("secret").tokenValiditySeconds(86400))
        .csrf(Customizer.withDefaults())                              // required for sessions
        .build();
}

// -------- TOKEN-BASED (SPAs, mobile, microservices) --------
@Bean SecurityFilterChain jwtChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
        .csrf(c -> c.disable())                                       // no sessions = no CSRF
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a
            .requestMatchers("/auth/**").permitAll()
            .anyRequest().authenticated())
        .oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))  // validates Bearer JWTs
        .build();
}

// Issuing a JWT on login
@PostMapping("/auth/login")
public Map<String, String> login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
    Authentication auth = authManager.authenticate(
        new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(req.username(), req.password()));
    Instant now = Instant.now();
    JwtClaimsSet claims = JwtClaimsSet.builder()
        .issuer("self").issuedAt(now).expiresAt(now.plus(15, ChronoUnit.MINUTES))
        .subject(auth.getName())
        .claim("scope", auth.getAuthorities().stream().map(Object::toString).toList())
        .build();
    String token = jwtEncoder.encode(JwtEncoderParameters.from(claims)).getTokenValue();
    return Map.of("access_token", token, "token_type", "Bearer");
}
```

| Aspect | Session | JWT |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Server state | `HttpSession` per user | none |
| Scaling | sticky session or Redis store | trivially horizontal |
| Revocation | delete session row | denylist token until expiry |
| Size on wire | ~32-byte cookie | ~500-byte header per request |
| CSRF risk | yes (cookies auto-sent) | low if stored in memory, high if in cookies |
| Best fit | server-rendered apps | SPA, mobile, service-to-service |

- **Sessions** shine when you can keep server-side state and want instant revocation — e-commerce checkout, admin portals.
- **JWTs** shine when requests cross service boundaries and you want zero lookup cost — APIs, mobile, microservices.
- Do not store JWTs in `localStorage` if you can avoid it — a single XSS flaw leaks the token. Prefer `HttpOnly` cookies with `SameSite=Strict` or in-memory storage with silent refresh.
- Either way, keep access-token lifetimes short (15 min) and rotate with refresh tokens.

**Target: ~100ms per hash** on production hardware. Fast enough that login latency is acceptable; slow enough that offline brute force is impractical. Revisit yearly as hardware improves.

**Benchmark BCrypt cost on production-equivalent hardware:**

```java
for (int cost = 10; cost <= 16; cost++) {
    BCryptPasswordEncoder encoder = new BCryptPasswordEncoder(cost);
    long start = System.nanoTime();
    encoder.encode("benchmark-password");
    long elapsed = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;
    System.out.printf("Cost %d: %dms%n", cost, elapsed);
    if (elapsed >= 100) break;
}
```

**Argon2id sensible defaults:**
- Memory: 64MB
- Iterations: 3
- Parallelism: 2 threads
- Use `Argon2PasswordEncoder.defaultsForSpringSecurity_v5_8()` then benchmark

**Production `DelegatingPasswordEncoder`:**

```java
@Bean
public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
    Map<String, PasswordEncoder> encoders = new HashMap<>();
    encoders.put("bcrypt", new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12));
    encoders.put("argon2", Argon2PasswordEncoder.defaultsForSpringSecurity_v5_8());
    return new DelegatingPasswordEncoder("argon2", encoders);
}
```

**Lazy migration via `UserDetailsPasswordService`:**
- User logs in with `{bcrypt}...` hash
- `DelegatingPasswordEncoder.upgradeEncoding()` returns `true`
- Spring re-hashes with Argon2 and calls `updatePassword(UserDetails, newHash)`
- You persist the new hash; next login uses Argon2
- No user action, no bulk script, no downtime
